  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Sense of Brutality shows early scholarly uptake and clear evidence of classroom and readership interest. The book has 3 citations, 1 recorded teaching-adoption signal through a New Books Network discussion, and 1 library holding, alongside 1,654 OAPEN downloads. Its reach also extends to an open-access and policy-relevant frame, with alignment to Peace, Justice and strong institutions.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Carlos S\u00e1nchez\u2019s A Sense of Brutality, published by Amherst College Press in 2020, is beginning to circulate across scholarship, teaching, and open-access reading communities. The citation record is still small, but it is real and distributed across different years and publication types, indicating that the book is entering academic conversation rather than remaining isolated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest usage signal comes from downloads: the book has been accessed 1,654 times through OAPEN, suggesting substantial reader interest in the open-access edition. That pattern is consistent with the book\u2019s availability through hybrid open access and with its presence in major open repositories. Library and discovery records also show at least one holding, and the book is associated with subject areas such as sociology, drug traffic, drug control, organized crime, and violence.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching and public-facing uptake is visible as well. The book is discussed in a New Books Network en espa\u00f1ol video, which points to use in broader intellectual and educational settings. This kind of signal does not count as citation impact, but it does show that the work is being introduced to audiences beyond specialist researchers.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape suggests interdisciplinary resonance. The citing works include an ethics article, a book chapter on peace in Medell\u00edn, and a discourse-analysis article on narcoculture in Chile. Taken together, these citations indicate that the book is being taken up in conversations about philosophy, political and social analysis, peacebuilding, and discourse, which fits the book\u2019s thematic focus on brutality, violence, and interpretation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also points to policy relevance through its alignment with Peace, Justice and strong institutions. Overall, the evidence shows an early but meaningful profile: modest scholarly citation, stronger reader uptake, visible educational engagement, and open-access distribution that is likely helping the book travel across disciplines and audiences.</p>",
